    difficulty: 'moderate',
    difficultyNotes: 'The route combines exposed Oneida Lake shoreline and canal flatwater with a deceptive breakwater, heavy summer boat and wake traffic, swimmer and water-toy conflicts, side-channel access, Wood Creek debris, Lock E22’s large lift, closed-bridge logistics, private marinas, and a hard Lock E21 boundary.',
    confidenceNotes: 'High corridor confidence: the official Canalway stewardship inventory publishes the distinct 6.1-mile Section 18 and its launch chain. The official guidebook supplies the breakwater warning, Verona Beach and Squires access, facilities, Oneida Lake/canal hazards, camping, and Lock E22/E21 boundary context. Current NYSDEC material independently confirms the Cove Road public accessible launch. USGS 04237496 is a distant same-basin proxy and no numeric threshold is asserted.',
    gaugeStatusNote: 'USGS 04237496 is on the Seneca River near Baldwinsville, west of this east-Oneida-Lake corridor, and is used only as broad basin trend context. Local lake wind, waves, water level, traffic, fog, temperature, shoreline conditions, and lock operations can differ materially.',
    safetyNotes: [
      'Wear a fitted PFD and carry a communication plan, spare paddle, light, food, sun protection, and a cold-water layer. Keep clear of swimmers, fishing lines, tow ropes, personal watercraft, and resort traffic.',
      'Use USGS 04237496 as broad proxy context only. No numeric runnable band is published for Section 18; inspect local wind, waves, weather, fog, traffic, water level, debris, water temperature, and every access before launch.',
      'At the Sylvan Beach breakwater, stay well south of marker R106 as directed by the guide. Do not cut across the jetty or approach it from the lake without a clear visual of the channel and boat traffic.',
      'Treat Lock E22 and Lock E21 as navigation and portage controls. Stay clear of gates, spillways, and lock hydraulics; verify a legal carry and never assume a floating dock authorizes passage.',
      'Oneida Lake wind and waves can build rapidly and there may be no easy landing once committed. Take the conservative shoreline option, turn back early, and do not continue beyond the Section 18 boundary without a separate route and shuttle plan.',
    ],
    shuttle: 'Stage the downstream vehicle at a confirmed Lock E21/Section 18 take-out or use Cove Road/another verified bailout, then drive to Verona Beach or Squires Landing. Allow time for the long shuttle, summer resort congestion, lake wind checks, lock/bridge constraints, and an early retreat margin.',
    camping: 'Verona Beach State Park provides the strongest documented camping option near the route start, with 46 campsites and New York State Parks registration/reservation requirements. Nearby Fish Creek campgrounds have dedicated docks but are separate managed properties; reserve directly. No dispersed camping is claimed at Squires, the village beach, Cove Road, locks, or canal banks.',
    campingClassification: 'endpoint_campground',
    permits: 'Confirm current NYS Parks registration and launch rules, Village of Sylvan Beach beach/parking/swimming restrictions, NYSDEC Cove Road rules, Canal Corporation lock and bridge notices, private marina permission, boating/PFD requirements, and clean-drain-dry procedures before departure.',
    accessCaveats: [
      'The stored coordinates follow official Erie Canalway guide anchors and current NYSDEC Cove Road access data; dock, ramp, beach, parking, carry, traffic, water, and seasonal conditions remain subject to current local operation.',
      'The official stewardship inventory lists four launch contexts while also identifying Lock E22/E21 controls. This record keeps the documented public launch chain and hard boundary visible without treating private marinas or lock structures as unrestricted access.',
      'Squires Landing is on a side channel, and the Sylvan Beach hand-launch context is subject to village beach rules. Confirm the actual current water entry, swimmer restrictions, carry, and parking before relying on either.',
      'Cove Road is a strong current public access point but opens onto the Oneida Lake/Barge Canal environment. Treat its accessible ramp as access evidence, not as a guarantee of calm lake conditions or permission to continue through the next section.',
    ],
